      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but can u be more specific about the people involved? like, in a typical case of a neighborhood being revitalized, what are the main types of people moving in, and what kind of residents are usually the ones leaving? just trying to get a feel for the demographic shifts that happen during these cycles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In typical revitalization cases, wealthier professionals, young families, and often first-generation homebuyers tend to move into replacing older, long-term residents who are often working-class or low-income. This demographic shift usually correlates with higher exposure to elevated property values and the creation of exclusive amenities that can be barriers to entry for previous neighbors. While the influx of these residents often brings increased investment, the specific group leaving is frequently those unable to keep pace with rising costs or those whose local businesses no longer serve the new, higher-income clientele. Ultimately, sociologists study these patterns to distinguish between genuine community upgrading where all classes share uplift, versus displacement where existing populations are pushed out entirely.",
